.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt SQUEUE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm squeue_setup ,
.Nm squeue_enter ,
.Nm squeue_register
.Nd shared-queue asynchronous I/O
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/io_uring.h
.Ft int
.Fn squeue_setup "unsigned entries" "struct io_uring_params *params"
.Ft int
.Fo squeue_enter
.Fa "int fd" "unsigned to_submit" "unsigned min_complete"
.Fa "unsigned flags" "const void *arg" "size_t argsz"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn squeue_register "int fd" "unsigned op" "void *arg" "unsigned nr_args"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm squeue
.Pq "shared queue"
is the native
.Fx
completion-based asynchronous I/O interface.
An application submits I/O operations and reaps their results through a pair of
ring buffers shared between the kernel and the process, so that many operations
can be submitted with a single system call and their completions collected with
none.
It is the native analogue of the Linux
.Em io_uring
interface and shares the same on-ring wire format; the Linux
.Em io_uring
compatibility layer and these native system calls are two front ends over one
kernel engine, in the same relationship that
.Xr kqueue 2
bears to Linux
.Em epoll .
.Pp
.Fn squeue_setup
creates a ring able to hold at least
.Fa entries
submission entries and returns a file descriptor referring to it.
.Fa params
is both an input and an output argument: on input its
.Fa flags
select ring options; on output the kernel fills in the sizes of the
submission and completion queues
.Pq Fa sq_entries , cq_entries ,
the set of supported features
.Pq Fa features ,
and the byte offsets
.Pq Fa sq_off , cq_off
of each ring field.
The supported setup flags are
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQSIZE ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CLAMP ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_NO_SQARRAY ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SUBMIT_ALL ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SINGLE_ISSUER ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_R_DISABLED ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_COOP_TASKRUN ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_TASKRUN_FLAG ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_DEFER_TASKRUN ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQE128 ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE32 ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE_MIXED ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQ_AFF ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_NO_MMAP ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_REGISTERED_FD_ONLY ,
and
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQ_REWIND .
With
.Dv IORING_SETUP_NO_SQARRAY ,
the submission-index array is omitted and
.Fa sq_off.array
is zero.
The consumer uses the masked submission head as the SQE index;
applications publish SQEs in that order before advancing the submission tail.
With
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SUBMIT_ALL ,
submission continues after an SQE preparation error.
Without it, a failed terminal SQE ends the submission batch; later entries
remain queued for the next call.
Execution errors do not end the batch.
An invalid submission-array index is dropped without counting a submitted
SQE and ends the batch in either mode.
.Pp
With
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SINGLE_ISSUER ,
only the creating thread may submit entries or register resources.
Other threads may wait for completions without submitting.
Ownership survives exec and cannot be reassigned when the owner exits.
For rings created with
.Dv IORING_SETUP_R_DISABLED ,
ownership is instead assigned to the thread that enables the ring.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_SETUP_COOP_TASKRUN
and
.Dv IORING_SETUP_DEFER_TASKRUN
match the shared engine's existing rule that resolved asynchronous work is run
when an owner enters the kernel.
.Dv IORING_SETUP_TASKRUN_FLAG
sets
.Dv IORING_SQ_TASKRUN
while such work is pending and clears it after enter drains the ready list.
TASKRUN_FLAG requires COOP_TASKRUN or DEFER_TASKRUN, and DEFER_TASKRUN requires
SINGLE_ISSUER.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQE128
uses 128-byte submission slots; the standard first 64 bytes retain their normal
layout and the extension is left available to opcodes that define it.
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE32
uses 32-byte completion slots and zeros the extra 16 bytes for ordinary
completions.
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE_MIXED
uses ordinary 16-byte slots and lets operations that request an extended
completion consume two consecutive slots.
It is incompatible with
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE32
and requires at least two completion slots.
An extended completion sets
.Dv IORING_CQE_F_32 ;
if it would straddle the ring boundary, a zero
.Dv IORING_CQE_F_SKIP
entry pads the last slot and the extended completion begins at slot zero.
With
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQ_REWIND ,
which requires
.Dv IORING_SETUP_NO_SQARRAY ,
each enter consumes up to the requested count from SQE index zero without
reading or modifying the shared SQ head and tail.
.Pp
With
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL ,
a kernel thread in the creating process consumes published submission entries.
After the idle interval in
.Fa sq_thread_idle
.Pq one second when zero ,
the thread may sleep and set
.Dv IORING_SQ_NEED_WAKEUP .
The application then uses
.Dv IORING_ENTER_SQ_WAKEUP
to resume submission.
.Dv IORING_ENTER_SQ_WAIT
waits for space in a full submission ring.
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQ_AFF
requires SQPOLL and pins that thread to the online CPU numbered by
.Fa sq_thread_cpu ;
the CPU must be allowed by the creating process's cpuset.
The thread is pinned before setup returns successfully.
SQPOLL cannot be combined with
.Dv IORING_SETUP_COOP_TASKRUN ,
.Dv IORING_SETUP_TASKRUN_FLAG ,
or
.Dv IORING_SETUP_DEFER_TASKRUN .
.Pp
A disabled ring rejects enter calls until
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_ENABLE_RINGS
is registered with a null argument and zero count.
Resources may be registered before enabling.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_RESTRICTIONS
installs one array of
.Vt struct io_uring_restriction
while the ring is disabled; enabling activates the policy permanently.
A failed installation leaves the ring unrestricted and permits retry.
The policy allows specified SQE and registration opcodes, and specifies
allowed and required SQE flags.
Required flags are implicitly allowed.
Repeated flag rules replace the preceding rule of the same kind.
Unlisted operations are denied.
A non-null array with zero entries installs a policy denying all operations.
.Pp
Other setup flags are rejected.
Unless
.Dv IORING_SETUP_NO_MMAP
selects caller-owned pinned ring memory, three regions are mapped into the
process with
.Xr mmap 2
on the returned descriptor at the offsets
.Dv IORING_OFF_SQ_RING ,
.Dv IORING_OFF_CQ_RING ,
and
.Dv IORING_OFF_SQES .
On this implementation the submission and completion rings share a single
mapping
.Pq Dv IORING_FEAT_SINGLE_MMAP .
.Pp
To submit work the application writes one or more
.Vt struct io_uring_sqe
submission entries into the SQE array, publishes their indices through the
submission ring's tail, and calls
.Fn squeue_enter .
Each completed operation yields a
.Vt struct io_uring_cqe
completion entry in the completion ring carrying the submission's
.Fa user_data
and a result
.Fa res
.Pq a non-negative byte count or other value on success, or a negative errno .
Unknown SQE flag bits and nonzero unregistered personalities produce an
.Dv EINVAL
completion before the request is issued.
.Fa to_submit
is the number of new submissions to consume;
.Fa min_complete ,
together with
.Dv IORING_ENTER_GETEVENTS
in
.Fa flags ,
is the number of completions to wait for before returning.
Completions may be read directly from the shared ring without any further
system call.
.Pp
When waiting with
.Dv IORING_ENTER_GETEVENTS ,
.Fa arg
may point to a native
.Vt sigset_t
and
.Fa argsz
must be its size.
The mask temporarily replaces the calling thread's signal mask during the wait;
the original mask is restored on return,
after delivery of an interrupting signal.
A null pointer leaves the mask unchanged.
.Pp
With
.Dv IORING_ENTER_EXT_ARG ,
.Fa arg
instead points to a
.Vt struct io_uring_getevents_arg
and
.Fa argsz
must equal that structure's size.
Its
.Fa sigmask
and
.Fa sigmask_sz
fields describe an optional native signal mask.
Its
.Fa ts
field is an optional pointer to a
.Vt struct __kernel_timespec
containing a relative timeout, or an absolute
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
deadline if
.Dv IORING_ENTER_ABS_TIMER
is set.
The
.Fa min_wait_usec
field must be zero.
Support is advertised by
.Dv IORING_FEAT_EXT_ARG ;
.Dv IORING_FEAT_MIN_TIMEOUT
is not advertised.
The wait arguments are ignored without
.Dv IORING_ENTER_GETEVENTS .
A wait timeout leaves outstanding operations active and does not generate a CQE.
.Dv IORING_ENTER_NO_IOWAIT
is accepted as an execution-accounting hint.
The shared engine does not classify its waits as Linux block-I/O waits, so the
flag does not change scheduling or completion behavior.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_NOP
accepts the Linux 6.18 NOP option set.
.Dv IORING_NOP_INJECT_RESULT
returns the signed 32-bit value from
.Fa len .
.Dv IORING_NOP_FILE
validates
.Fa fd ;
.Dv IORING_NOP_FIXED_FILE
selects a registered-file slot for that validation, and is ignored when
.Dv IORING_NOP_FILE
is absent.
.Dv IORING_NOP_FIXED_BUFFER
validates the registered buffer named by
.Fa buf_index .
.Dv IORING_NOP_TW
is accepted and completes through the shared submission context.
.Dv IORING_NOP_CQE32
returns the two 64-bit extension words supplied in
.Fa off
and
.Fa addr
when the ring uses
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE32
or
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQE_MIXED .
A mixed-layout completion sets
.Dv IORING_CQE_F_32 ;
a fixed 32-byte completion does not.
Without either setup mode the flag returns
.Er EINVAL .
Unknown NOP flags also return
.Er EINVAL
before descriptor or buffer lookup.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_FIXED_FD_INSTALL
installs a held registered file in the caller's ordinary descriptor table and
preserves the capability rights captured at registration time.
The new descriptor has close-on-exec set by default.
.Dv IORING_FIXED_FD_NO_CLOEXEC
disables close-on-exec; every other install flag returns
.Er EINVAL
before fixed-slot lookup.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_PIPE
returns ordinary pipe descriptors through the address named by
.Fa addr
when
.Fa file_index
is zero.
A nonzero explicit index installs the two pipe ends in consecutive
registered-file slots beginning at the one-based index;
.Dv IORING_FILE_INDEX_ALLOC
selects two empty slots from the configured allocation range and copies their
zero-based indices to
.Fa addr .
Direct output rejects
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
If the second installation or result copyout fails, newly installed ends are
removed and affected slots remain empty, including slots whose previous contents
were replaced earlier in the operation.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_LINK_TIMEOUT
must follow a linked request and name one readable
.Vt struct __kernel_timespec .
Its timestamp is copied before the chain starts; the timer arms when the
preceding request begins execution.
Completion of that request cancels the deadline with
.Er ECANCELED .
Expiry attempts to cancel the predecessor and reports
.Er ETIMEDOUT
on native rings, or Linux ETIME on Linux rings, when cancellation is accepted.
Failure to cancel reports the corresponding error.
Completion order between a target and its deadline is unspecified.
A hard link permits subsequent requests to continue after failure.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL
matches the target's
.Fa user_data .
With
.Dv IORING_ASYNC_CANCEL_ALL ,
its result is the number of matching cancellation requests accepted, including
zero when none matched.
Without that flag a missing target reports
.Er ENOENT .
.Dv IORING_OP_TIMEOUT_REMOVE
applies to ordinary timeouts;
neither operation removes a linked deadline by its own key.
Other cancellation matching modes and timeout updates remain unsupported.
.Pp
Cancellation of executing I/O retains its request, file and buffer ownership
until the executor finishes.
Interruptible sleeps are interrupted; uninterruptible I/O may continue until
it can return safely, and a completed or partial transfer keeps its byte count.
Releasing the last ring file reference requests cancellation of outstanding
work.
Closing one descriptor alias leaves work owned by another alias active.
.Pp
Read and write requests validate
.Va rw_flags
before issuing I/O.
.Dv IORING_RWF_DSYNC
and
.Dv IORING_RWF_SYNC
select per-write synchronous completion through the filesystem write path.
.Dv IORING_RWF_APPEND
appends one write;
.Dv IORING_RWF_NOAPPEND
ignores the descriptor's append flag for one write.
Neither changes the descriptor's flags.
Combining the two returns
.Er EINVAL .
These write policies have no effect on reads.
Unknown flags and the currently unsupported NOWAIT, ATOMIC, DONTCACHE and
NOSIGNAL policies return
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
HIPRI requires an IOPOLL ring and currently returns
.Er EINVAL .
The same policy applies to inline requests, worker requests and registered
files and buffers.
.Pp
.Fn squeue_register
associates auxiliary resources with a ring to make them cheaper or safer to
use across many operations.
.Fa op
selects the operation and
.Fa arg / Fa nr_args
describe its argument.
Supported operations include
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_BUFFERS
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_BUFFERS
.Pq fixed buffers for READ_FIXED / WRITE_FIXED and READV_FIXED / WRITEV_FIXED ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES2 ,
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_FILES ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_UPDATE ,
and
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_UPDATE2
.Pq fixed files, referenced from a submission by index with IOSQE_FIXED_FILE ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD_ASYNC ,
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_EVENTFD
.Pq completion notification through Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_CLOCK
.Pq select the clock for absolute completion waits ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_RESIZE_RINGS
.Pq replace the submission and completion ring storage ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_PERSONALITY
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_PERSONALITY
.Pq capture and release credentials selected by Va personality ,
and
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_PROBE
.Pq query which operations the kernel supports .
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_RESIZE_RINGS
requires a ring created with
.Dv IORING_SETUP_DEFER_TASKRUN .
.Fa arg
points to a
.Vt struct io_uring_params
and
.Fa nr_args
must be one.
Only
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CQSIZE
and
.Dv IORING_SETUP_CLAMP
may be supplied as new flags; the original ring layout is inherited.
The call returns the new sizes and offsets through the parameter structure.
A resize that cannot hold pending submissions or completions fails with
.Er EOVERFLOW
and leaves the old ring active.
After success, callers must map the new ring and SQE regions again.
Old mappings remain readable but are detached from the new ring.
.Pp
A fixed file is remembered with the capsicum rights the descriptor held at the
time it was registered, and an operation performed through it can never exceed
those rights.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_PROBE
reports the operations implemented by the ring's front end.
Native rings advertise core operations; Linux compatibility rings additionally
advertise their Linux ABI extensions.
An operation's presence does not imply support for every optional mode.
The entire input probe buffer, including its operation entries, must be zero.
.Fa nr_args
must not exceed 256, and
.Fa arg
must be non-null even when
.Fa nr_args
is zero.
The kernel returns at most the number of operation entries it defines and
leaves bytes beyond the returned structure unchanged.
Malformed input is rejected without writing an output probe.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_PERSONALITY
requires a null argument and a zero count, captures the caller's current
credentials and returns an identifier from 1 through 65535.
A submission with that identifier in
.Va personality
executes under the captured credentials.
The credential reference is acquired during submission preparation, so
unregistering the identifier does not alter a request that is already prepared
or executing.
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_PERSONALITY
takes the identifier in
.Fa nr_args ,
requires a null argument and rejects zero, unknown and out-of-range identifiers.
Ring destruction releases every remaining registration.
The policy is identical for inline operations and asynchronous workers.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_CLOCK
sets the clock used by absolute
.Dv IORING_ENTER_EXT_ARG
completion waits on that ring.
Native rings accept
.Dv CLOCK_UPTIME
and
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ;
Linux compatibility rings translate Linux
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
and
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME
at the front-end boundary.
Relative waits and timeout SQEs are unaffected.
.Fa nr_args
must be zero, all reserved words must be zero, and rejected registrations leave
the current selection unchanged.
The default is the uptime clock.
.Ss Event-loop integration
A ring descriptor is a first-class event source.
It may be passed to
.Xr poll 2
or registered with
.Xr kqueue 2
using
.Dv EVFILT_READ ,
becoming ready whenever unreaped completions are present, or an
.Xr eventfd 2
may be registered with
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD
to be signalled whenever a completion is published to the CQ.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD_ASYNC
instead signals only when a shared squeue file-I/O worker places resolved work
on the ring's ready queue; the event loop then calls
.Fn squeue_enter
to publish that completion in the ring owner's context.
Inline completions do not signal an asynchronous registration.
Setting
.Dv IORING_CQ_EVENTFD_DISABLED
in the shared CQ flags suppresses either notification mode until the bit is
cleared.
The registration retains the eventfd after its original descriptor is closed.
Only one eventfd mode may be registered at a time.
Any of the three event mechanisms lets a ring be multiplexed alongside other
descriptors in an existing event loop.
.Ss Capability mode
The
.Nm
system calls are permitted in capability mode
.Pq Xr cap_enter 2 .
Every operation is subject to the capsicum rights of the descriptor it acts on.
In addition, a ring created by a process in capability mode may operate only on
.Em fixed
files registered with
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES ;
an operation naming a raw descriptor, or one that resolves a name in the
ambient file-system namespace, fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE .
A ring is thus a closed, rights-limited set of capabilities that a sandboxed
process may drive asynchronously.
.Pp
Registered buffers hold writable pages and their backing objects until the
registration and every issued request using it have been released.
Unmapping or replacing a user mapping does not redirect fixed-buffer I/O.
Every vector must lie within the buffer selected by
.Va buf_index ;
a different registered slot does not authorize that vector.
Missing slots and out-of-range accesses fail with
.Er EFAULT .
Unregistering detaches the table; requests already using it retain the old
pages, including requests waiting for readiness or a worker.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_OP_PROVIDE_BUFFERS
and
.Dv IORING_OP_REMOVE_BUFFERS
manage bounded groups of application-provided buffers.
The shared engine can detach and return an ordered, contiguous batch while
preserving each buffer's original capacity across a readiness retry.
Linux-specific SEND/RECV bundle policy, option validation, completion flags,
and
.Dv IORING_FEAT_RECVSEND_BUNDLE
advertisement are supplied only by the Linux front end.
Native rings share the buffer-group lifetime and rollback mechanism but do not
advertise that Linux-only feature bit.
.Pp
Registrations accept up to 1024 LP64 iovecs, with at most 1 GiB per nonempty
buffer.
A null base with zero length denotes an empty slot.
Page references, including overlapping registrations, are charged against the
registering real user's aggregate
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
limit and the system-wide wired-page cap.
Explicitly lowered limits also apply to privileged callers.
The registration retains its owner's address space until the table retires.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_BUFFERS2
accepts a
.Vt struct io_uring_rsrc_register ,
including sparse-table creation and one optional 64-bit tag per buffer.
An empty slot must have a zero tag.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_BUFFERS_UPDATE
accepts a
.Vt struct io_uring_rsrc_update2
and replaces consecutive slots, returning the number processed.
A later error returns the completed prefix count while leaving the failing slot
and all later slots unchanged.
Each successful replacement publishes a new immutable buffer generation.
A tagged generation emits an auxiliary completion with its tag as
.Va user_data
and a zero result when its final reference retires.
Replacement or unregister therefore delays the tag completion while an issued
fixed-buffer request still owns that generation and its pinned pages.
Failed initial registration emits no tag completions.
Support is advertised with
.Dv IORING_FEAT_RSRC_TAGS .
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_CLONE_BUFFERS
copies a whole registered-buffer table or a selected source range from another
ring without pinning or charging the pages again.
The source ring may be named by an ordinary descriptor or by a task-local
registered-ring index.
The two rings must have the same creating real user and address space.
A cloned slot receives a new untagged resource generation that shares the
pinned backing; removing the source generation therefore emits its tag without
waiting for the clone to retire.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_DST_REPLACE
allows replacement of an existing destination table.
Slots before the destination offset are retained, the selected range is
replaced, and later old slots are cleared.
Clone registration and every other resource-table mutation are serialized as a
single transaction across the source and destination rings.
.Pp
Registered-file tables reject ring descriptors, including duplicates and other
rings, with
.Er EBADF .
Initial registration is atomic: an invalid descriptor or user-memory fault
leaves no table installed.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES2
accepts a
.Vt struct io_uring_rsrc_register ,
including sparse-table creation and one optional 64-bit tag per file.
Support is advertised with
.Dv IORING_FEAT_RSRC_TAGS .
An empty slot must have a zero tag.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_UPDATE ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_UPDATE2 ,
and
.Dv IORING_OP_FILES_UPDATE
replace consecutive slots, returning the number processed.
The extended update accepts one tag for each replacement.
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_SKIP
preserves a slot and a descriptor value of \-1 clears it.
A later error returns the completed prefix count.
An invalid descriptor clears its destination slot; a user-memory fault leaves
that slot unchanged.
Later slots remain unchanged in either case.
A tagged generation emits an auxiliary completion with its tag as
.Va user_data
and a zero result when its final reference retires.
Replacement or unregister therefore delays the tag completion while an issued
fixed-file request still owns that generation.
Failed initial registration emits no tag completions.
Unregister and all update paths serialize with acquisition of fixed-file
references and their captured capability rights.
Fixed-file requests forced through the asynchronous worker pool retain both the
selected generation and its captured rights until worker completion.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_RING_FDS
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_RING_FDS
manage 16 task-local held ring references.
Each
.Vt struct io_uring_rsrc_update
selects an explicit index or requests the first free index with an offset of
.Dv UINT32_MAX ;
the chosen index is copied back and successful prefixes are returned when a
later record fails.
.Dv IORING_ENTER_REGISTERED_RING
uses such an index for
.Fn squeue_enter ,
and
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_USE_REGISTERED_RING
uses one for another registration command.
Closing the ordinary descriptor does not destroy a registered ring, while task
exit releases all remaining registered references through deferred file-close
work.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_IOWQ_MAX_WORKERS
accepts exactly two
.Vt uint32_t
values for the ring's bounded and unbounded worker classes.
A nonzero value replaces that class's admission limit; zero leaves it
unchanged.
On success, the previous pair is copied back to the same address.
The update takes effect before that final copyout, so a copyout fault does not
roll it back.
Each initial limit is the current value of
.Va kern.squeue.max_workers .
Per-ring admission cannot cause the shared pool to exceed the system-wide
ceiling.
.Pp
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_IOWQ_AFF
sets the CPU mask used while workers execute requests for that ring.
.Fa nr_args
is the mask size in bytes; sizes larger than the native CPU-set representation
are clamped.
The mask must be nonempty and contained in the registering thread's allowed CPU
set.
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_IOWQ_AFF
restores unrestricted worker placement for the ring and is idempotent.
It requires a null argument and zero
.Fa nr_args .
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following variables control and report system-wide
.Nm
resource use:
.Bl -tag -width Va
.It Va kern.squeue.max_workers
Maximum number of asynchronous file-I/O worker processes shared by all rings.
The default is 8 and the accepted range is 1 through 256.
This variable is both a boot-time tunable and writable at runtime.
Reducing it prevents further pool growth but does not terminate workers that
already exist.
.It Va kern.squeue.workers
Current number of worker processes.
This variable is read-only.
.It Va kern.squeue.idle_workers
Current number of worker processes waiting for work.
This variable is read-only.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn squeue_setup
returns a new file descriptor on success.
.Fn squeue_enter
returns the number of submission entries consumed.
A positive submission count takes precedence over a subsequent wait error.
An interrupted or expired wait succeeds if any completion is available,
even if fewer than
.Fa min_complete
completions are ready.
.Fn squeue_register
returns 0 on success, except file-table updates return the processed count.
.Pp
On failure each returns \-1 and sets
.Va errno .
Note that a
.Em completion
error is delivered as a negative errno in the completion entry's
.Fa res
field and does not cause the submitting call to fail.
.Sh ERRORS
These system calls may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa entries
is zero or too large, an unsupported
.Fa flags
bit or register
.Fa op
was given, or a reserved argument was non-zero.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A completion wait was interrupted by a signal.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
A completion wait expired with no submissions consumed
and no completions available.
The Linux io_uring front end reports Linux ETIME for this condition.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa params
or a submission argument points outside the process's address space.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not a valid descriptor, an enter call used a disabled ring, or a
restriction/enable call used an already enabled ring.
The Linux front end reports Linux EBADFD for invalid ring state.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
A thread other than the single issuer attempted submission or registration.
.It Bq Er EACCES
The active restriction policy denies the operation or SQE flags.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
does not refer to a
.Nm
ring.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
a ring resize would discard pending submissions or completions.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
the ring's wired memory would exceed
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
or the system-wide limit
.Pq Va kern.squeue.max_wired_pages .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
a resource of the requested kind is already registered.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
in capability mode, an operation named a descriptor or namespace outside the
ring's registered, rights-limited set.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aio_read 2 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
interface first appeared in 5BSD.
Its on-ring format is compatible with the Linux
.Em io_uring
interface, which the 5BSD Linux compatibility layer exposes through the
.Fn io_uring_setup ,
.Fn io_uring_enter ,
and
.Fn io_uring_register
system calls over the same kernel engine.
